course: dictionary
lesson: 6117
title: The microscope
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A microscope is a science tool with lenses that makes very small things look much bigger. Your eyes alone cannot see cells or germs, but a microscope can show them to you.
explain: In class you place a slide on the stage, look through the lenses, and turn the focus knobs until the picture is sharp. Onion skin, pond water, hair and leaves all look different under a microscope.
explain: Context helps you here. «Germs are living things far too small to see without a microscope» tells you a microscope lets us see things our eyes cannot. Swap in your meaning: "far too small to see without a tool that makes tiny things bigger" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: A microscope is an instrument with lenses that makes very small things look much bigger.
fact: «Germs are living things far too small to see without a microscope», such as bacteria and viruses.
fact: Microscopes helped scientists discover cells and bacteria.
fact: «Put under the microscope» is a phrase that means studied very closely.
fact: Special microscopes can now map single atoms, although nobody has seen an atom with bare eyes.
exercise: What does microscope mean? | A large telescope for looking at stars | A bag for carrying science books | *A tool with lenses that makes very small things look much bigger
exercise: «Germs are living things far too small to see without a microscope.» What does a microscope let us do? | *See things too small for our eyes alone | Make bread rise faster | Hear very quiet sounds | Cook food more quickly
exercise: In a science lesson, what do you place on the stage of a microscope? | A football | *A slide with a sample on it | A bag of crisps | A map of the world
test: «Most viruses are much smaller than bacteria and too small to be seen with an ordinary light microscope.» What does this tell us about viruses? | They are bigger than bacteria | They are as big as a dog | *They are smaller than bacteria | They are easy to see with bare eyes
test: A scientist wants to see the tiny cells in onion skin. Which tool should she use? | A thermometer | *A microscope | A set of kitchen scales | A ruler
exam: Which phrase means to study something very closely? | Look at the sky | Put in the bin | Leave on the shelf | *Put under the microscope
